  "worked_solution": "This is a two-step random process: first, an integer $n$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $301,$ and then a number is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ We are asked to find the expected value of the number selected in the second step.\n\n- Let $Y$ be the first random number, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $301.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the player’s guess, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{1+Y}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[1+Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+\\text{E}[Y])\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,301\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1+301}{2}=151.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+\\text{E}[Y]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+151) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 152 \\\\  & =76.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
